window.SEARCH_INDEX = [{"url": "01-introduction-to-ai-startups.html#what-makes-a-startup-an-ai-startup", "chapter": "Ch 1 · Introduction to AI Startups", "section": "1.1 What makes a startup an AI startup", "title": "What makes a startup an AI startup", "text": "1.1What makes a startup an AI startup# Start with a test, because “we use AI” is now true of a spreadsheet. A company is an AI startup when a probabilistic model - one that generates, predicts, classifies, or reasons over open-ended inputs - is the load-bearin"}, {"url": "01-introduction-to-ai-startups.html#the-2026-ai-landscape-at-a-glance", "chapter": "Ch 1 · Introduction to AI Startups", "section": "1.2 The 2026 AI landscape at a glance", "title": "The 2026 AI landscape at a glance", "text": "1.2The 2026 AI landscape at a glance# You are founding into the largest capital reallocation in the history of technology. Global venture funding hit a record $510 billion in the first half of 2026, surpassing all of 2025, and AI absorbed the lion’s share - ro"}, {"url": "01-introduction-to-ai-startups.html#founder-market-fit-in-the-age-of-models", "chapter": "Ch 1 · Introduction to AI Startups", "section": "1.3 Founder-market fit in the age of models", "title": "Founder-market fit in the age of models", "text": "1.3Founder-market fit in the age of models# Product-market fit is Chapter 4’s territory. The prior question - founder-market fit - decides whether you will survive long enough to find it, and models have quietly rewritten what it requires. When any founder can"}, {"url": "01-introduction-to-ai-startups.html#the-new-lean-loop-build-measure-learn-with-models", "chapter": "Ch 1 · Introduction to AI Startups", "section": "1.4 The new lean loop: build-measure-learn with models", "title": "The new lean loop: build-measure-learn with models", "text": "1.4The new lean loop: build-measure-learn with models# The lean-startup loop - build, measure, learn - still governs, but models bend every arc of it, and getting the new shape right is the operating system of an AI startup. Build changes from writing logic to"}, {"url": "01-introduction-to-ai-startups.html#why-ai-value-accrues-to-products-not-models", "chapter": "Ch 1 · Introduction to AI Startups", "section": "1.5 Why AI value accrues to products, not models", "title": "Why AI value accrues to products, not models", "text": "1.5Why AI value accrues to products, not models# Here is the thesis the rest of the book defends, and the single most important belief a 2026 founder can hold: the enduring economic value in AI accrues to products, not to the base models underneath them. This "}, {"url": "02-ai-fundamentals-models.html#from-tokens-to-transformers-for-founders", "chapter": "Ch 2 · AI Fundamentals I: Models, Modalities & Inference", "section": "2.1 From tokens to transformers, for founders", "title": "From tokens to transformers, for founders", "text": "2.1From tokens to transformers, for founders# A language model does exactly one thing: given a sequence of tokens, it predicts a probability distribution over the next token. Everything else — chat, code, agents — is scaffolding around that single primitive, r"}, {"url": "02-ai-fundamentals-models.html#modalities-text-vision-audio-multimodal", "chapter": "Ch 2 · AI Fundamentals I: Models, Modalities & Inference", "section": "2.2 Modalities: text, vision, audio, multimodal", "title": "Modalities: text, vision, audio, multimodal", "text": "2.2Modalities: text, vision, audio, multimodal# A modality is a type of data the model can consume or produce: text, images, audio, video. The 2026 frontier is natively multimodal — a single model that ingests and reasons across text, images, and audio in one "}, {"url": "02-ai-fundamentals-models.html#open-vs-closed-dense-vs-mixture-of-experts-reasoning-models", "chapter": "Ch 2 · AI Fundamentals I: Models, Modalities & Inference", "section": "2.3 Open vs closed, dense vs mixture-of-experts, reasoning models", "title": "Open vs closed, dense vs mixture-of-experts, reasoning models", "text": "2.3Open vs closed, dense vs mixture-of-experts, reasoning models# You are not choosing “an AI.” You are choosing a point in a three-axis taxonomy, and each axis independently moves cost, control, and capability. (Which specific provider and model to sign with "}, {"url": "02-ai-fundamentals-models.html#context-windows-tokens-and-the-economics-of-inference", "chapter": "Ch 2 · AI Fundamentals I: Models, Modalities & Inference", "section": "2.4 Context windows, tokens and the economics of inference", "title": "Context windows, tokens and the economics of inference", "text": "2.4Context windows, tokens and the economics of inference# The context window is the maximum number of tokens — input plus output — the model can attend to at once. Frontier and leading open models now reach 1M tokens.3 A million-token window sounds like an in"}, {"url": "02-ai-fundamentals-models.html#latency-throughput-and-where-the-compute-goes", "chapter": "Ch 2 · AI Fundamentals I: Models, Modalities & Inference", "section": "2.5 Latency, throughput and where the compute goes", "title": "Latency, throughput and where the compute goes", "text": "2.5Latency, throughput and where the compute goes# Users feel latency; your CFO feels throughput. Both are governed by the two-phase structure of inference, and this is the section most founders skip and later regret. An inference request runs in two phases wi"}, {"url": "03-ai-fundamentals-rag-agents.html#prompting-as-an-engineering-discipline", "chapter": "Ch 3 · AI Fundamentals II: Prompting, RAG, Agents & Tooling", "section": "3.1 Prompting as an engineering discipline", "title": "Prompting as an engineering discipline", "text": "3.1Prompting as an engineering discipline# The phrase “prompt engineering” got a bad reputation in 2023 because it looked like superstition — magic incantations traded on forums. In 2026 it is a real discipline, and it has a new name that has largely stuck: co"}, {"url": "03-ai-fundamentals-rag-agents.html#retrieval-augmented-generation-in-one-page", "chapter": "Ch 3 · AI Fundamentals II: Prompting, RAG, Agents & Tooling", "section": "3.2 Retrieval-augmented generation in one page", "title": "Retrieval-augmented generation in one page", "text": "3.2Retrieval-augmented generation in one page# A model’s weights are frozen at its training cutoff and know nothing proprietary. Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) fixes both problems by fetching relevant text at request time and placing it in the context wi"}, {"url": "03-ai-fundamentals-rag-agents.html#tool-use-and-function-calling", "chapter": "Ch 3 · AI Fundamentals II: Prompting, RAG, Agents & Tooling", "section": "3.3 Tool use and function calling", "title": "Tool use and function calling", "text": "3.3Tool use and function calling# Retrieval gets facts into the model. Tool use — also called function calling — lets the model take actions and pull live data out of the world. You describe a set of functions to the model as structured schemas (name, descript"}, {"url": "03-ai-fundamentals-rag-agents.html#agents-loops-planning-and-when-to-use-them", "chapter": "Ch 3 · AI Fundamentals II: Prompting, RAG, Agents & Tooling", "section": "3.4 Agents: loops, planning, and when to use them", "title": "Agents: loops, planning, and when to use them", "text": "3.4Agents: loops, planning, and when to use them# Stack retrieval and tools inside a loop and you have an agent. The defining property of an agent is that the model, not your control flow, decides what happens next — it plans a step, acts via a tool, observes "}, {"url": "03-ai-fundamentals-rag-agents.html#structured-output-guardrails-and-failure-modes", "chapter": "Ch 3 · AI Fundamentals II: Prompting, RAG, Agents & Tooling", "section": "3.5 Structured output, guardrails and failure modes", "title": "Structured output, guardrails and failure modes", "text": "3.5Structured output, guardrails and failure modes# Everything above assumes the model’s output can be trusted by the rest of your system. It can’t be, not by default — a model emits text, and text is a terrible interface for software. The fix is structured ou"}, {"url": "04-markets-users-product-strategy.html#problem-solution-fit-for-ai-value-versus-gimmick", "chapter": "Ch 4 · Markets, Users & AI Product Strategy", "section": "4.1 Problem-solution fit for AI: value versus gimmick", "title": "Problem-solution fit for AI: value versus gimmick", "text": "4.1Problem-solution fit for AI: value versus gimmick# The foundational shift of the AI era is that capability is no longer the scarce resource. When GPT-class reasoning is a metered API call, the question “can software do this?” almost always answers yes. The "}, {"url": "04-markets-users-product-strategy.html#segmenting-for-ai-who-feels-the-pain-enough", "chapter": "Ch 4 · Markets, Users & AI Product Strategy", "section": "4.2 Segmenting for AI: who feels the pain enough", "title": "Segmenting for AI: who feels the pain enough", "text": "4.2Segmenting for AI: who feels the pain enough# AI does not change whether you need a sharp ICP - it changes which segment converts. The winning segment has three properties: the work is high-volume and repetitive (so automation compounds), the cost of the st"}, {"url": "04-markets-users-product-strategy.html#product-market-fit-signals-for-ai-products", "chapter": "Ch 4 · Markets, Users & AI Product Strategy", "section": "4.3 Product-market-fit signals for AI products", "title": "Product-market-fit signals for AI products", "text": "4.3Product-market-fit signals for AI products# The classic PMF instruments still apply, but AI products throw off two dangerous false positives. First, demo euphoria. AI products get abnormally strong early traction because the demo is magic - and then churn h"}, {"url": "04-markets-users-product-strategy.html#the-thin-wrapper-trap-and-where-to-add-real-value", "chapter": "Ch 4 · Markets, Users & AI Product Strategy", "section": "4.4 The thin-wrapper trap and where to add real value", "title": "The thin-wrapper trap and where to add real value", "text": "4.4The thin-wrapper trap and where to add real value# A thin wrapper is a product whose entire value is a prompt plus a UI over a foundation model’s API, with nothing underneath. The economics are brutal: analyses in 2026 found that a majority of AI wrapper st"}, {"url": "04-markets-users-product-strategy.html#ai-business-models-and-usage-based-pricing", "chapter": "Ch 4 · Markets, Users & AI Product Strategy", "section": "4.5 AI business models and usage-based pricing", "title": "AI business models and usage-based pricing", "text": "4.5AI business models and usage-based pricing# Pricing is where AI product strategy becomes a business. The historical default - per-seat SaaS - is actively breaking, because the entire promise of an AI agent is to do the work of seats. Charging per human seat"}, {"url": "05-data-strategy-flywheel.html#data-as-the-ai-moat", "chapter": "Ch 5 · Data Strategy & the Data Flywheel", "section": "5.1 Data as the AI moat", "title": "Data as the AI moat", "text": "5.1Data as the AI moat# Model quality is converging and renting it is trivial. When your competitor can call the same Claude, Gemini, or GPT endpoint you can, the base intelligence is table stakes. The durable asset is proprietary feedback data from real users"}, {"url": "05-data-strategy-flywheel.html#acquiring-and-labeling-data-including-synthetic", "chapter": "Ch 5 · Data Strategy & the Data Flywheel", "section": "5.2 Acquiring and labeling data, including synthetic", "title": "Acquiring and labeling data, including synthetic", "text": "5.2Acquiring and labeling data, including synthetic# Before the flywheel spins on its own, you have a cold-start problem: you need enough data to make the product good enough that people will use it and generate more. There are four levers, and mature teams pu"}, {"url": "05-data-strategy-flywheel.html#designing-the-data-flywheel", "chapter": "Ch 5 · Data Strategy & the Data Flywheel", "section": "5.3 Designing the data flywheel", "title": "Designing the data flywheel", "text": "5.3Designing the data flywheel# The flywheel is not a metaphor you hope emerges; it is an engineering system you design. The canonical loop has four stages, each of which you must instrument deliberately. .dg text{font-family:var(--sans);fill:var(--ink);font-s"}, {"url": "05-data-strategy-flywheel.html#capturing-feedback-and-implicit-signals", "chapter": "Ch 5 · Data Strategy & the Data Flywheel", "section": "5.4 Capturing feedback and implicit signals", "title": "Capturing feedback and implicit signals", "text": "5.4Capturing feedback and implicit signals# Training data quality lives or dies at the capture layer, and most teams under-invest here catastrophically. There are two families of signal, and you need both. Explicit feedback is what the user deliberately tells "}, {"url": "05-data-strategy-flywheel.html#privacy-governance-and-data-rights", "chapter": "Ch 5 · Data Strategy & the Data Flywheel", "section": "5.5 Privacy, governance and data rights", "title": "Privacy, governance and data rights", "text": "5.5Privacy, governance and data rights# Here is where founders quietly sabotage their own flywheel: they build the loop and only later discover they never had the right to train on the data flowing through it. Data you cannot legally use for training is not fu"}, {"url": "06-customer-validation-for-ai.html#why-ai-needs-a-new-validation-playbook", "chapter": "Ch 6 · Customer Validation for AI", "section": "6.1 Why AI needs a new validation playbook", "title": "Why AI needs a new validation playbook", "text": "6.1Why AI needs a new validation playbook# Classical customer development assumes the product does what the spec says: if the button exists and the wire is connected, the feature works. AI breaks that assumption. Your system is non-deterministic, its quality i"}, {"url": "06-customer-validation-for-ai.html#building-an-eval-set-that-mirrors-reality", "chapter": "Ch 6 · Customer Validation for AI", "section": "6.2 Building an eval set that mirrors reality", "title": "Building an eval set that mirrors reality", "text": "6.2Building an eval set that mirrors reality# An eval set is a curated collection of inputs paired with a definition of a good output. Its single most important property is representativeness: the distribution of cases in your set must match the distribution y"}, {"url": "06-customer-validation-for-ai.html#offline-vs-online-evals-human-in-the-loop", "chapter": "Ch 6 · Customer Validation for AI", "section": "6.3 Offline vs online evals; human-in-the-loop", "title": "Offline vs online evals; human-in-the-loop", "text": "6.3Offline vs online evals; human-in-the-loop# Evals come in two regimes, and confusing them is a classic founder error. Offline evals run against your frozen set, on demand, in CI — fast, cheap, reproducible, comparative. They are the right tool for gating a "}, {"url": "06-customer-validation-for-ai.html#llm-as-judge-rubrics-and-inter-rater-reliability", "chapter": "Ch 6 · Customer Validation for AI", "section": "6.4 LLM-as-judge, rubrics and inter-rater reliability", "title": "LLM-as-judge, rubrics and inter-rater reliability", "text": "6.4LLM-as-judge, rubrics and inter-rater reliability# You cannot afford to have humans grade every candidate on every change — that is the cost that kills offline iteration. The 2026 workhorse is LLM-as-judge: a strong model scores each output against a writte"}, {"url": "06-customer-validation-for-ai.html#red-teaming-and-validating-trust-before-launch", "chapter": "Ch 6 · Customer Validation for AI", "section": "6.5 Red-teaming and validating trust before launch", "title": "Red-teaming and validating trust before launch", "text": "6.5Red-teaming and validating trust before launch# A high resolution rate validates capability. It does not validate trust, and for an autonomous agent trust is the thing that actually gets bought. Cortex acts on its own — it reads untrusted customer text and "}, {"url": "07-choosing-model-providers.html#the-build-versus-buy-spectrum", "chapter": "Ch 7 · Choosing Model Providers & Hugging Face", "section": "7.1 The build-versus-buy spectrum", "title": "The build-versus-buy spectrum", "text": "7.1The build-versus-buy spectrum# There is no single “build or buy” switch. There is a spectrum of ownership, and each step to the right buys you control at the price of capital, talent, and time. .dg text { font-family: var(--sans, sans-serif); fill: var(--in"}, {"url": "07-choosing-model-providers.html#choosing-a-frontier-model-provider", "chapter": "Ch 7 · Choosing Model Providers & Hugging Face", "section": "7.2 Choosing a frontier model provider", "title": "Choosing a frontier model provider", "text": "7.2Choosing a frontier model provider# If you’re renting, you’re choosing among a handful of frontier labs whose flagship models cluster within a few dozen Elo points of one another on public leaderboards.33The public yardstick most founders cite is Arena (the"}, {"url": "07-choosing-model-providers.html#the-open-weight-route-and-hugging-face", "chapter": "Ch 7 · Choosing Model Providers & Hugging Face", "section": "7.3 The open-weight route and Hugging Face", "title": "The open-weight route and Hugging Face", "text": "7.3The open-weight route and Hugging Face# The moment your workload is high-volume, latency-sensitive, privacy-constrained, or simply expensive at frontier prices, the open-weight route earns a serious look. In 2026 the open frontier is genuinely strong: Qwen "}, {"url": "07-choosing-model-providers.html#the-inference-layer-sets-your-margins", "chapter": "Ch 7 · Choosing Model Providers & Hugging Face", "section": "7.4 The inference layer sets your margins", "title": "The inference layer sets your margins", "text": "7.4The inference layer sets your margins# Choosing an open-weight model is only half the decision; you still have to serve it. This is where founders who “went open to save money” sometimes discover they’ve traded a predictable API bill for an unpredictable GP"}, {"url": "07-choosing-model-providers.html#putting-it-together-route-dont-marry", "chapter": "Ch 7 · Choosing Model Providers & Hugging Face", "section": "7.5 Putting it together: route, don’t marry", "title": "Putting it together: route, don’t marry", "text": "7.5Putting it together: route, don’t marry# The mature 2026 pattern isn’t “pick the one model.” It’s a portfolio: a router that sends each request to the cheapest model that clears your quality bar, escalates hard cases, and fails over when a provider degrades"}, {"url": "08-content-data-stack.html#embeddings-and-chunking-done-right", "chapter": "Ch 8 · The Content & Data Stack", "section": "8.1 Embeddings and chunking done right", "title": "Embeddings and chunking done right", "text": "8.1Embeddings and chunking done right# An embedding turns a span of text into a vector so that semantically similar spans land near each other. The chunk — the unit of text you embed and later retrieve — is the single most consequential design decision in the "}, {"url": "08-content-data-stack.html#choosing-a-vector-database-or-store", "chapter": "Ch 8 · The Content & Data Stack", "section": "8.2 Choosing a vector database or store", "title": "Choosing a vector database or store", "text": "8.2Choosing a vector database or store# The market has fragmented into good options, so the wrong-answer surface has shrunk — but the “defensible default” question is sharper than ever. Do not start by asking which vector database is best. Ask how many vectors"}, {"url": "08-content-data-stack.html#rag-architecture-patterns-in-production", "chapter": "Ch 8 · The Content & Data Stack", "section": "8.3 RAG architecture patterns in production", "title": "RAG architecture patterns in production", "text": "8.3RAG architecture patterns in production# A demo RAG pipeline is embed(query) → top-k → prompt. A production one is a graph with an ingestion side and a serving side that share nothing but the index, and the diagram below is the spine every serious system co"}, {"url": "08-content-data-stack.html#data-pipelines-freshness-and-sync", "chapter": "Ch 8 · The Content & Data Stack", "section": "8.4 Data pipelines, freshness and sync", "title": "Data pipelines, freshness and sync", "text": "8.4Data pipelines, freshness and sync# The demo indexes a corpus once. The product indexes a corpus that changes — help articles get edited, policies get rewritten, tickets get resolved — and here is the failure mode nobody sees coming: semantic similarity has"}, {"url": "08-content-data-stack.html#reranking-hybrid-search-and-retrieval-quality", "chapter": "Ch 8 · The Content & Data Stack", "section": "8.5 Reranking, hybrid search and retrieval quality", "title": "Reranking, hybrid search and retrieval quality", "text": "8.5Reranking, hybrid search and retrieval quality# Pure vector search has a blind spot that surprises people: it does not know words. Dense embeddings capture meaning, so they nail “automobile repair” ≈ “car maintenance” — but they fumble the exact tokens that"}, {"url": "09-infra-observability-llmops.html#llmops-vs-mlops-vs-devops", "chapter": "Ch 9 · Infrastructure, Observability & LLMOps", "section": "9.1 LLMOps vs MLOps vs DevOps", "title": "LLMOps vs MLOps vs DevOps", "text": "9.1LLMOps vs MLOps vs DevOps# Every generation of software operations inherited the last one’s tools and then found a failure mode the old tools were blind to. DevOps made deploys fast, reversible, and observable, but it assumed the code you shipped was the co"}, {"url": "09-infra-observability-llmops.html#prompt-and-config-versioning-and-deploys", "chapter": "Ch 9 · Infrastructure, Observability & LLMOps", "section": "9.2 Prompt and config versioning and deploys", "title": "Prompt and config versioning and deploys", "text": "9.2Prompt and config versioning and deploys# A prompt is code. It has inputs, control flow (few-shot examples, conditionals baked into templates), a runtime contract with the model, and it breaks silently when edited. Yet the default failure of early-stage AI "}, {"url": "09-infra-observability-llmops.html#observability-tracing-logs-token-and-cost-metrics", "chapter": "Ch 9 · Infrastructure, Observability & LLMOps", "section": "9.3 Observability: tracing, logs, token and cost metrics", "title": "Observability: tracing, logs, token and cost metrics", "text": "9.3Observability: tracing, logs, token and cost metrics# You cannot debug what you cannot see, and an LLM request is nearly opaque by default: a string goes in, a string comes out, and everything that determined quality — which prompt version, which retrieved "}, {"url": "09-infra-observability-llmops.html#caching-routing-and-fallbacks-in-production", "chapter": "Ch 9 · Infrastructure, Observability & LLMOps", "section": "9.4 Caching, routing and fallbacks in production", "title": "Caching, routing and fallbacks in production", "text": "9.4Caching, routing and fallbacks in production# Between your application and the frontier providers belongs exactly one component: an LLM gateway. It puts a single OpenAI-compatible endpoint in front of many providers and centralizes caching, routing, fallbac"}, {"url": "09-infra-observability-llmops.html#reliability-rate-limits-and-incident-readiness", "chapter": "Ch 9 · Infrastructure, Observability & LLMOps", "section": "9.5 Reliability, rate limits and incident readiness", "title": "Reliability, rate limits and incident readiness", "text": "9.5Reliability, rate limits and incident readiness# AI is now infrastructure, and it fails like infrastructure — except the failures are stranger. 2026 made this concrete: on June 2, Anthropic’s Claude had a major global disruption with elevated error rates ac"}, {"url": "10-production-evals-monitoring.html#from-pre-launch-evals-to-continuous-evals", "chapter": "Ch 10 · Production Evals & Monitoring", "section": "10.1 From pre-launch evals to continuous evals", "title": "From pre-launch evals to continuous evals", "text": "10.1From pre-launch evals to continuous evals# A pre-launch eval runs once against a curated dataset and answers “is this good enough to ship?” A continuous eval runs forever against a sample of real traffic and answers a harder question: “is this still good e"}, {"url": "10-production-evals-monitoring.html#monitoring-quality-drift-and-regressions", "chapter": "Ch 10 · Production Evals & Monitoring", "section": "10.2 Monitoring quality, drift and regressions", "title": "Monitoring quality, drift and regressions", "text": "10.2Monitoring quality, drift and regressions# Standard observability — latency, error rate, token spend (Chapter 9) — will not detect a quality collapse. Your p99 can be flat, your 5xx rate zero, your costs nominal, and your agent can still be silently giving"}, {"url": "10-production-evals-monitoring.html#guardrails-and-hallucination-detection-in-production", "chapter": "Ch 10 · Production Evals & Monitoring", "section": "10.3 Guardrails and hallucination detection in production", "title": "Guardrails and hallucination detection in production", "text": "10.3Guardrails and hallucination detection in production# Evals tell you, after the fact, that quality dropped. Guardrails act in the request path, in real time, to stop a bad output before the user sees it. They are complementary: evals are your smoke detecto"}, {"url": "10-production-evals-monitoring.html#surviving-provider-and-model-updates", "chapter": "Ch 10 · Production Evals & Monitoring", "section": "10.4 Surviving provider and model updates", "title": "Surviving provider and model updates", "text": "10.4Surviving provider and model updates# Here is the industry’s dirtiest open secret, and the reason continuous evals are non-negotiable for an AI startup: the model behind your API call can change without you doing anything. Even when you pin a version, the "}, {"url": "10-production-evals-monitoring.html#closing-the-loop-production-signals-into-the-eval-set", "chapter": "Ch 10 · Production Evals & Monitoring", "section": "10.5 Closing the loop: production signals into the eval set", "title": "Closing the loop: production signals into the eval set", "text": "10.5Closing the loop: production signals into the eval set# A continuous-eval system that only alerts is a system that decays. Every regression it catches, every hallucination a guardrail blocks, every ticket a human overturns is a new test case your offline s"}, {"url": "11-selling-ai-customer-success.html#the-ai-buyer-is-skeptical-earn-it-back", "chapter": "Ch 11 · Selling AI & Customer Success", "section": "11.1 The AI Buyer Is Skeptical — Earn It Back", "title": "The AI Buyer Is Skeptical — Earn It Back", "text": "11.1The AI Buyer Is Skeptical — Earn It Back# The default emotional state of a 2026 AI buyer is burned optimism. They believe AI can help; they do not believe your AI can help their messy reality. Disarming this is not a pitch technique, it is a sequence of co"}, {"url": "11-selling-ai-customer-success.html#proving-roi-pilots-pocs-and-the-success-bar", "chapter": "Ch 11 · Selling AI & Customer Success", "section": "11.2 Proving ROI: Pilots, POCs, and the Success Bar", "title": "Proving ROI: Pilots, POCs, and the Success Bar", "text": "11.2Proving ROI: Pilots, POCs, and the Success Bar# Skepticism is disarmed with words; it is closed with evidence. The POC is the center of gravity of the entire AI sales motion, and a badly designed POC is worse than none — it burns weeks, produces ambiguous "}, {"url": "11-selling-ai-customer-success.html#surviving-the-ai-security-review", "chapter": "Ch 11 · Selling AI & Customer Success", "section": "11.3 Surviving the AI Security Review", "title": "Surviving the AI Security Review", "text": "11.3Surviving the AI Security Review# In 2026 the security review is no longer a formality that legal handles after the handshake — it is a hard gate that opens the commercial conversation, and it has grown an AI-specific second head. Enterprise vendor-risk te"}, {"url": "11-selling-ai-customer-success.html#pricing-conversations-and-procurement", "chapter": "Ch 11 · Selling AI & Customer Success", "section": "11.4 Pricing Conversations and Procurement", "title": "Pricing Conversations and Procurement", "text": "11.4Pricing Conversations and Procurement# You designed your pricing model back in strategy (see Chapter 4); this section is about the pricing conversation — surviving the moment procurement enters the room and reframes your carefully justified ROI as a line i"}, {"url": "11-selling-ai-customer-success.html#customer-success-retention-for-ai-products", "chapter": "Ch 11 · Selling AI & Customer Success", "section": "11.5 Customer Success & Retention for AI Products", "title": "Customer Success & Retention for AI Products", "text": "11.5Customer Success & Retention for AI Products# Closing the deal is the start of the risk, not the end of it. AI products churn differently and faster than traditional SaaS, and the same autonomy that makes them easy to adopt makes them easy to cancel. Below"}, {"url": "12-defensibility-ai-moat.html#why-we-have-ai-is-not-a-moat", "chapter": "Ch 12 · Defensibility & the AI Moat", "section": "12.1 Why “we have AI” is not a moat", "title": "Why “we have AI” is not a moat", "text": "12.1Why “we have AI” is not a moat# Start by burying the most seductive lie in your pitch deck. Access to a great model is not an advantage, because access is the one thing every competitor also has. The same three API keys that power Cortex, our running examp"}, {"url": "12-defensibility-ai-moat.html#the-real-moats-data-workflow-distribution-switching-costs", "chapter": "Ch 12 · Defensibility & the AI Moat", "section": "12.2 The real moats: data, workflow, distribution, switching costs", "title": "The real moats: data, workflow, distribution, switching costs", "text": "12.2The real moats: data, workflow, distribution, switching costs# Strip away the hype and roughly five durable moats survive in the AI era: proprietary data that compounds with usage, workflow integration so deep that removal is surgery, distribution you own "}, {"url": "12-defensibility-ai-moat.html#deep-tech-and-proprietary-model-moats-when-they-are-real", "chapter": "Ch 12 · Defensibility & the AI Moat", "section": "12.3 Deep-tech and proprietary-model moats — when they are real", "title": "Deep-tech and proprietary-model moats — when they are real", "text": "12.3Deep-tech and proprietary-model moats — when they are real# There is a romantic founder belief that the only real moat is your own model. Sometimes true, usually not, and the difference is worth a company. The commoditization of the base reasoning layer is"}, {"url": "12-defensibility-ai-moat.html#network-effects-and-the-data-flywheel-as-defense", "chapter": "Ch 12 · Defensibility & the AI Moat", "section": "12.4 Network effects and the data flywheel as defense", "title": "Network effects and the data flywheel as defense", "text": "12.4Network effects and the data flywheel as defense# Network effects are the strongest moat in software because they make your product get better for every user as a direct function of having more users — a dynamic a competitor cannot buy past without first m"}, {"url": "12-defensibility-ai-moat.html#compounding-advantage-over-time-cortexs-flywheel", "chapter": "Ch 12 · Defensibility & the AI Moat", "section": "12.5 Compounding advantage over time — Cortex’s flywheel", "title": "Compounding advantage over time — Cortex’s flywheel", "text": "12.5Compounding advantage over time — Cortex’s flywheel# A moat is not a static wall you finish building; it is a rate of change. The right question is not “is Cortex defensible today?” but “does the gap between Cortex and a fresh competitor widen every quarte"}, {"url": "13-finance-unit-economics.html#ai-cogs-inference-not-just-servers", "chapter": "Ch 13 · Finance & Unit Economics for AI", "section": "13.1 AI COGS: inference, not just servers", "title": "AI COGS: inference, not just servers", "text": "13.1AI COGS: inference, not just servers# In a classic SaaS profit-and-loss statement, cost of goods sold (COGS) is hosting, a support team, and some third-party tooling — a thin, mostly fixed layer under a torrent of near-free revenue. In an AI company, COGS "}, {"url": "13-finance-unit-economics.html#unit-economics-cost-and-margin-per-action", "chapter": "Ch 13 · Finance & Unit Economics for AI", "section": "13.2 Unit economics: cost and margin per action", "title": "Unit economics: cost and margin per action", "text": "13.2Unit economics: cost and margin per action# Seat-based pricing is a lie for AI products, because a “seat” that fires 5,000 agent runs a month costs you fifty times more to serve than one that fires 100. The only honest unit of analysis is the action: one r"}, {"url": "13-finance-unit-economics.html#the-gross-margin-reality-of-ai-products", "chapter": "Ch 13 · Finance & Unit Economics for AI", "section": "13.3 The gross-margin reality of AI products", "title": "The gross-margin reality of AI products", "text": "13.3The gross-margin reality of AI products# Here is the uncomfortable table every AI founder should stare at. Gross margin is not a vanity metric; at exit it is a valuation multiplier, because a 70%-margin dollar of revenue is worth far more than a 50%-margin"}, {"url": "13-finance-unit-economics.html#burn-runway-and-compute-budgeting", "chapter": "Ch 13 · Finance & Unit Economics for AI", "section": "13.4 Burn, runway and compute budgeting", "title": "Burn, runway and compute budgeting", "text": "13.4Burn, runway and compute budgeting# AI startups burn differently from SaaS, and the difference is compute. GPU spend consumes 40–60% of technical budgets in the first two years, and AI startups burn roughly 2× what SaaS companies spend on infrastructure.2 "}, {"url": "13-finance-unit-economics.html#financial-modeling-and-the-metrics-investors-probe", "chapter": "Ch 13 · Finance & Unit Economics for AI", "section": "13.5 Financial modeling and the metrics investors probe", "title": "Financial modeling and the metrics investors probe", "text": "13.5Financial modeling and the metrics investors probe# Build your model bottom-up from the unit, never top-down from a TAM percentage. Start with cost and price per action, layer in volume growth, model the COGS curve bending down as routing and caching matur"}, {"url": "14-venture-funding-for-ai.html#the-2026-ai-funding-landscape", "chapter": "Ch 14 · Venture Funding for AI", "section": "14.1 The 2026 AI funding landscape", "title": "The 2026 AI funding landscape", "text": "14.1The 2026 AI funding landscape# The headline number lies. AI captured roughly 80% of venture dollars in early 2026 and nearly half of all global funding in 2025,2 but that aggregate is a distortion field created by a handful of mega-rounds. OpenAI closed on"}, {"url": "14-venture-funding-for-ai.html#what-ai-investors-actually-diligence", "chapter": "Ch 14 · Venture Funding for AI", "section": "14.2 What AI investors actually diligence", "title": "What AI investors actually diligence", "text": "14.2What AI investors actually diligence# The diligence checklist for an AI startup has diverged sharply from the classic SaaS one. A generalist investor five years ago wanted ARR, growth rate, net retention, and CAC payback. Those still matter. But an AI-lite"}, {"url": "14-venture-funding-for-ai.html#compute-credits-and-the-cost-of-the-raise", "chapter": "Ch 14 · Venture Funding for AI", "section": "14.3 Compute, credits and the cost of the raise", "title": "Compute, credits and the cost of the raise", "text": "14.3Compute, credits and the cost of the raise# Here is the fact that reframes how much you actually need to raise: an AI startup with a working product and a credible team can assemble hundreds of thousands of dollars in cloud compute credits before selling a"}, {"url": "14-venture-funding-for-ai.html#valuations-dilution-and-the-ai-premium", "chapter": "Ch 14 · Venture Funding for AI", "section": "14.4 Valuations, dilution and the AI premium", "title": "Valuations, dilution and the AI premium", "text": "14.4Valuations, dilution and the AI premium# The numbers first, because founders negotiate better when they know the medians. In 2026, pre-seed rounds land around $750K–$1.5M on a $4M–$6M post-money valuation, almost always on post-money SAFEs with caps near $"}, {"url": "14-venture-funding-for-ai.html#the-ai-pitch-narrative-and-the-data-story", "chapter": "Ch 14 · Venture Funding for AI", "section": "14.5 The AI pitch narrative and the data story", "title": "The AI pitch narrative and the data story", "text": "14.5The AI pitch narrative and the data story# Everything above converges on the pitch. In 2026 an AI fundraise is won or lost on a single question the investor is really asking behind all the others: why does this get more defensible over time instead of less"}, {"url": "15-pitfalls-crises-ai.html#the-ai-specific-failure-catalog", "chapter": "Ch 15 · Pitfalls & Crises in AI", "section": "15.1 The AI-Specific Failure Catalog", "title": "The AI-Specific Failure Catalog", "text": "15.1The AI-Specific Failure Catalog# Traditional software fails deterministically: a bug is a bug, a null is a null, a fix is permanent. AI systems fail probabilistically and non-locally. The same input produces a good answer on Tuesday and a lawsuit on Wednes"}, {"url": "15-pitfalls-crises-ai.html#model-regressions-and-the-overnight-quality-drop", "chapter": "Ch 15 · Pitfalls & Crises in AI", "section": "15.2 Model Regressions and the Overnight Quality Drop", "title": "Model Regressions and the Overnight Quality Drop", "text": "15.2Model Regressions and the Overnight Quality Drop# This failure mode has no analog in classical software, so founders are least prepared for it. You wake up, nothing in your repo changed, and your product is measurably dumber. The cause is almost always ups"}, {"url": "15-pitfalls-crises-ai.html#hallucination-and-safety-incidents", "chapter": "Ch 15 · Pitfalls & Crises in AI", "section": "15.3 Hallucination and Safety Incidents", "title": "Hallucination and Safety Incidents", "text": "15.3Hallucination and Safety Incidents# A regression degrades quality. A hallucination or safety incident degrades trust - and increasingly, exposes you to liability. The legal ground shifted hard, and every AI founder needs the new posture: courts now hold co"}, {"url": "15-pitfalls-crises-ai.html#provider-dependency-and-deprecation", "chapter": "Ch 15 · Pitfalls & Crises in AI", "section": "15.4 Provider Dependency and Deprecation", "title": "Provider Dependency and Deprecation", "text": "15.4Provider Dependency and Deprecation# Your model provider is simultaneously your most critical infrastructure and a vendor you cannot audit, cannot SLA into submission, and cannot stop from changing under you. This dependency shows up as three distinct cris"}, {"url": "15-pitfalls-crises-ai.html#crisis-response-the-pivot-and-post-mortems", "chapter": "Ch 15 · Pitfalls & Crises in AI", "section": "15.5 Crisis Response, the Pivot and Post-Mortems", "title": "Crisis Response, the Pivot and Post-Mortems", "text": "15.5Crisis Response, the Pivot and Post-Mortems# Naming failures is table stakes. What separates companies that survive AI crises is a rehearsed response loop. When Cortex’s resolution rate cratered at 02:14, the difference between a two-hour incident and a tw"}, {"url": "16-team-culture-leadership.html#the-ai-org-research-engineering-and-product-as-one-triangle", "chapter": "Ch 16 · Team, Culture & Leadership for AI", "section": "16.1 The AI org: research, engineering, and product as one triangle", "title": "The AI org: research, engineering, and product as one triangle", "text": "16.1The AI org: research, engineering, and product as one triangle# The classic startup org has two poles — engineering and product — with design and go-to-market hanging off them. The AI org adds a third, applied research (or applied AI), and the defining cha"}, {"url": "16-team-culture-leadership.html#hiring-and-retaining-scarce-ai-talent", "chapter": "Ch 16 · Team, Culture & Leadership for AI", "section": "16.2 Hiring and retaining scarce AI talent", "title": "Hiring and retaining scarce AI talent", "text": "16.2Hiring and retaining scarce AI talent# Plan around the market’s shape, not wish it away. The 2026 ManpowerGroup survey of 39,000 employers has 72% reporting talent shortages, with AI skills the hardest capability to find for the first time, displacing trad"}, {"url": "16-team-culture-leadership.html#a-culture-of-experimentation-and-evals", "chapter": "Ch 16 · Team, Culture & Leadership for AI", "section": "16.3 A culture of experimentation and evals", "title": "A culture of experimentation and evals", "text": "16.3A culture of experimentation and evals# The cultural core of an AI company is that evals are the unit of truth, not opinions, demos, or the founder’s gut. The mechanics of eval design and production monitoring belong to Chapters 6 and 10; here the concern "}, {"url": "16-team-culture-leadership.html#leading-through-model-driven-uncertainty", "chapter": "Ch 16 · Team, Culture & Leadership for AI", "section": "16.4 Leading through model-driven uncertainty", "title": "Leading through model-driven uncertainty", "text": "16.4Leading through model-driven uncertainty# The hardest leadership problem in an AI company has no analog in ordinary software: your core dependency ships a new version on someone else’s schedule, and it can obsolete a quarter of your work or hand you a feat"}, {"url": "16-team-culture-leadership.html#responsible-ai-governance-inside-the-team", "chapter": "Ch 16 · Team, Culture & Leadership for AI", "section": "16.5 Responsible-AI governance inside the team", "title": "Responsible-AI governance inside the team", "text": "16.5Responsible-AI governance inside the team# The external dimensions of responsible AI — regulation, ethics frameworks, disclosure obligations, the EU AI Act, TRAIGA, California’s SB 53 — are Chapter 17’s territory. This section is strictly about the interna"}, {"url": "17-responsible-ai-regulation.html#responsibility-as-a-business-asset-not-a-tax", "chapter": "Ch 17 · Responsible AI: Safety, Ethics & Regulation", "section": "17.1 Responsibility as a business asset, not a tax", "title": "Responsibility as a business asset, not a tax", "text": "17.1Responsibility as a business asset, not a tax# Start with the economics, because the ethics follow the money more reliably than founders admit. Responsible AI produces three compounding returns. It shortens sales cycles: by mid-2026 the question “Are you I"}, {"url": "17-responsible-ai-regulation.html#bias-fairness-and-transparency-in-practice", "chapter": "Ch 17 · Responsible AI: Safety, Ethics & Regulation", "section": "17.2 Bias, fairness and transparency in practice", "title": "Bias, fairness and transparency in practice", "text": "17.2Bias, fairness and transparency in practice# Fairness is not a slogan you affirm; it is a number you can show. The failure mode that bites support agents is quality-of-service harm: the model answers competently in standard English and degrades for minorit"}, {"url": "17-responsible-ai-regulation.html#safety-misuse-and-human-oversight", "chapter": "Ch 17 · Responsible AI: Safety, Ethics & Regulation", "section": "17.3 Safety, misuse and human oversight", "title": "Safety, misuse and human oversight", "text": "17.3Safety, misuse and human oversight# Autonomy multiplies both value and hazard. In NIST red-team exercises, novel agent-tailored attacks achieved an 81% task-hijacking success rate against a tool-using agent, versus 11% for known baseline attacks - agents a"}, {"url": "17-responsible-ai-regulation.html#the-2026-regulatory-map-the-eu-ai-act-and-beyond", "chapter": "Ch 17 · Responsible AI: Safety, Ethics & Regulation", "section": "17.4 The 2026 regulatory map: the EU AI Act and beyond", "title": "The 2026 regulatory map: the EU AI Act and beyond", "text": "17.4The 2026 regulatory map: the EU AI Act and beyond# The regulatory picture in mid-2026 is a patchwork moving at different speeds, and founders who wait for it to settle will wait past their deployment date. Anchor on three regimes. The EU AI Act classifies "}, {"url": "17-responsible-ai-regulation.html#governance-documentation-and-audit-readiness", "chapter": "Ch 17 · Responsible AI: Safety, Ethics & Regulation", "section": "17.5 Governance, documentation and audit readiness", "title": "Governance, documentation and audit readiness", "text": "17.5Governance, documentation and audit readiness# Governance turns good intentions into evidence, and evidence is what survives a regulator, an enterprise security review, or a courtroom. The Air Canada tribunal in 2024 and the Hamm court in 2026 converged on"}];